---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { METRICS } from "../data/metrics";
const M = METRICS;
---
<Base title="recurrence model · FloodWatch.PH" pathname="/recurrence" description="Track B (the flood-prediction model): finds which places flood again and again, using a satellite fingerprint of the land and a simple classifier trained on 57 past Philippine floods, tested by holding out whole storms.">
  <article class="max-w-prose mx-auto px-6 pt-8 pb-12">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-500 mb-3 font-mono">Track B &middot; the flood-prediction model &middot; v1</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl mb-2">Which places flood again and again.</h1>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      Track A (the single-storm flood map) shows where the water went during one storm. Track B (the flood-prediction model) answers a different question: which places flood again and again, season after season? These are separate jobs.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
      Like SolarMap, Track B is a trained classifier on a fixed satellite fingerprint of the land. Anyone can re-run it and get the identical result. The rest of this page is the technical detail, with each precise term explained in plain words the first time.
    </p>

    <p class="text-[11px] text-ink-500 mb-8 leading-relaxed italic border-l-2 border-ink-300/40 pl-4">
      Observed flood extent derived from public satellite data. Patterns may have legitimate explanations; figures warrant independent verification.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">The satellite fingerprint of the land: Google AlphaEarth</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The satellite data the model learns from is Google AlphaEarth Foundations "Satellite Embedding V1" (<code>GOOGLE/SATELLITE_EMBEDDING/V1/ANNUAL</code>): a compact numeric summary of what each patch of land looks like from space. Natively 10 m, sampled here at <strong>300 m</strong> (averaged per cell, because repeated flooding is an area property), one summary per year 2017-2025, CC-BY-4.0 (produced by Google and Google DeepMind). The year is fixed at <strong>2017</strong>, the earliest available and the same period the Global Flood Database record ends.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      One summary per year is too coarse for a single storm (that is Track A's job) but well-suited to "does this place's land look like a place that floods repeatedly." The summaries are computed on Google's servers and downloaded as a small file; no bulk raw imagery is pulled.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">The classifier</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      A simple, well-understood classifier (<code>sklearn.linear_model.LogisticRegression</code>) on that fingerprint, calibrated so the score reads like a real probability. About 30 seconds to train on any laptop. No GPU. Anyone can re-run it and get the identical result: the same cached data file (<code>model/embeddings/floodwatch_embeddings_v1.npz</code>) always produces the same <code>recurrence_clf_v1.joblib</code>, checked by <code>make hash-verify</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">Training examples</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      "Flooded" examples: points inside past flood footprints from the Global Flood Database for 57 Philippine floods (2002-2017), with rivers and lakes removed (the same removal as Track A). "Not flooded" examples: land points that appear in none of those 57 floods.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The record covers 2002-2017. Floods since 2017 (Odette 2021, Paeng 2022, Carina 2024) are not in the training data. The model learns repeat-flood patterns from the past record; how well it carries to more recent flooding is an open question stated plainly in the methodology, not hidden.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">Tested by holding out whole storms</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The model is tested by holding out <em>whole storms</em>, never random points. Splitting on random points would cheat: two nearby points look almost identical, so the model would effectively see the test answers and the scores would look better than they are. The held-out storms are named in <code>model/holdout_events.json</code>; the split is pinned in the Makefile. An automated check asserts no held-out storm leaks into training.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      This is the single most important honesty decision in the project. The numbers reflect how the model does on storms it never trained on.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">How well it does</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The model was tested by holding out whole storms, never random points. The precision and recall on that held-out test, the calibration curve, and the full numbers are reported on the <a href="/methodology" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">methodology page</a>. The score is calibrated so it reads like a real probability.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-paper-alt border border-ink-300/40 p-4 mb-4 font-mono text-sm">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-y-1 gap-x-6 text-xs">
        <span class="text-ink-500">Tested by</span><span>Holding out whole storms</span>
        <span class="text-ink-500">Calibration</span><span>Calibrated to read like a probability</span>
        <span class="text-ink-500">Model fingerprint</span><span>{M.clf_sha256}</span>
        <span class="text-ink-500">Cutoff in use</span><span>{M.trackB_threshold}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-ink-500 leading-relaxed italic">
      A chart showing how well the score is calibrated is published with the project; the precision and recall figures are on the <a href="/methodology" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">methodology page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">What the output looks like</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The Track B output is <code>site/public/data/recurrence_prone.geojson</code>: points on a <strong>300 m</strong> grid over the area. Each point has a calibrated <code>score</code> (0-1) and a <code>prone</code> flag (score at or above the cutoff in use). No home identifiers. The points are a sample grid, not buildings.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The gap pipeline sums Track B scores up by province (average score per province) and compares against what past records show (Global Flood Database) to produce the <code>gap</code> bucket (<code>under_observed_prone</code> / <code>charted</code> / <code>monitored</code> / <code>low</code>). v1.0 does not compare against the official hazard maps; that comparison is a later step, held back because those government maps are locked behind logins or apps. A city and barangay breakdown is also a later refinement.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">What Track B does not claim</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
      <li>Track B scores how likely a place is to flood again, based on what its land looked like from space in 2017. It is not a water-flow model and does not account for new drainage, new flood walls, or land-cover changes after 2017.</li>
      <li>A high score does not mean a specific building will flood; it means the land there has been linked to flooding in the past. Province-level summing is fair; single-point claims are not.</li>
      <li>Track B and Track A measure different things and are not comparable. Do not average or combine their numbers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-3">Anyone can re-run it</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      <code>make train</code> runs offline from the cached data file. <code>make hash-verify</code> asserts the model matches the exact pinned fingerprint. <code>make calibrate</code> fits the calibration. All three give the identical result every time. Anyone with the repository can re-run the classifier without network access after the first <code>make embeddings</code> pull.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
